[THOUGHT] The patient has no medication exposure, no systemic or autoimmune history, no family history of neuromuscular disease, and no associated symptoms (bulbar, rash, myalgia). This makes acquired toxic or immune-mediated causes less likely — increasing suspicion for a primary myopathic process, possibly genetic or age-related (e.g., sporadic inclusion body myositis, limb-girdle muscular dystrophy, or mitochondrial myopathy). Before requesting lab/imaging tests, I must determine whether objective physical exam findings have already been documented — particularly proximal strength grading, presence of atrophy, fasciculations (which would suggest motor neuron involvement), or reflex changes (reduced in neuropathy or motor neuron disease; preserved in pure myopathy). Since the patient is reporting symptoms but we lack examiner-confirmed signs, the next highest-yield step is to request targeted physical exam findings — especially formal strength testing — to confirm pattern and severity.

[TEST] Please perform and report the results of a targeted neurological physical examination, specifically:  
- Manual muscle testing (MMT) graded 0–5 for bilateral hip flexion (iliopsoas), knee extension (quadriceps), shoulder abduction (deltoid), and elbow flexion (biceps);  
- Assessment for muscle atrophy in proximal limb muscles (thighs, shoulders);  
- Presence or absence of fasciculations in tongue, hands, or calves;  
- Patellar and biceps deep tendon reflexes (graded 0–4+).

